WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.232
Night-operation requirements vary by jurisdiction, aircraft, purpose, location, and operating framework.

00:00:06.232 --> 00:00:10.470
Check current official aviation sources before flight.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:20.193
Do not rely on an old checklist, a social post, or software geofencing as a complete legal determination.

00:00:20.193 --> 00:00:33.405
Requirements may address pilot qualification, aircraft registration or identification, anti-collision lighting, visual line of sight, airspace authorization, observers, records, and operations near people or vehicles.

00:00:33.405 --> 00:00:38.640
Local rules and property access may add separate obligations.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:46.368
Define the mission purpose, area, route, altitude strategy, required imagery, crew roles, and duration.

00:00:46.368 --> 00:00:56.091
Confirm the site is authorized and that collection will not extend into unnecessary private areas or sensitive locations.

00:00:56.091 --> 00:01:04.317
Review airspace, temporary restrictions, airports, emergency activity, obstacles, terrain, weather, temperature, lighting, and nearby operations.

00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:15.535
Visit or review the site in daylight when appropriate so hidden wires, branches, structures, and landing hazards are known before darkness.

00:01:15.535 --> 00:01:19.773
Set go or no-go and abort criteria.

00:01:19.773 --> 00:01:31.490
These may include minimum visibility, acceptable wind, battery condition, lighting function, navigation confidence, signal quality, crew readiness, and a clear recovery area.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.725
Use limits appropriate to official requirements and manufacturer guidance.

00:01:36.725 --> 00:01:46.447
Document the sources checked, approvals, aircraft, crew, weather, site findings, risk controls, return settings, emergency options, and decision.

00:01:46.447 --> 00:01:53.677
When authority or conditions are unclear, delay the flight and obtain qualified guidance.
